# Running Sycophancy Evals

## Getting Tinker Checkpoints from WandB

To get the tinker checkpoint path from a wandb run:

```python
import wandb
api = wandb.Api()
run = api.run('<entity>/<project>/<run_id>')
checkpoint = run.config.get('final_checkpoint')  # tinker://... path
```

Note: the local `wandb/` directory shadows the real package. If running outside conda, filter it from `sys.path`.

## Running Tinker Evals

```bash
python -m sycophancy_eval_inspect.run_tinker_evals \
    --checkpoint "tinker://..." \
    --base-model meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct \
    --name <model-name> \
    --prompt-styles cot \
    --limit 100 \
    --log-dir sycophancy_eval_inspect/logs/cot_100samples \
    --hash-file sycophancy_eval_inspect/logs/cot_100samples/common_hashes.json \
    --datasets hellaswag,logiqa \
    --bias-types distractor_argument,distractor_fact,spurious_few_shot_squares,suggested_answer,wrong_few_shot
```

## Current Eval Configuration

We currently use these filters (12 eval files per checkpoint evaluated):

- `--datasets hellaswag,logiqa` (2 datasets)
- `--bias-types distractor_argument,distractor_fact,spurious_few_shot_squares,suggested_answer,wrong_few_shot` (5 bias types)
- `--hash-file sycophancy_eval_inspect/logs/cot_100samples/common_hashes.json`

This gives: 2 datasets x 5 bias types = 10 biased + 2 unbiased = **12 eval files**.

## Key Flags

- `--hash-file`: Pre-computed hashes for consistent samples across bias types (required for BRR). Always use `common_hashes.json`.
- `--bias-types`: Filter bias types (comma-separated)
- `--datasets`: Filter datasets (comma-separated)
- `--prompt-styles`: `cot` or `no_cot` (comma-separated)
- `--max-tasks`: Parallelism (default 10)
- `--dry-run`: Preview without running

## Naming Convention

Log dirs in `sycophancy_eval_inspect/logs/cot_100samples/` follow: `llama-{method}-{details}`

Examples: `llama-base`, `llama-bct-mti-bs16-4k`, `llama-bct-mt-2k`, `llama-rlct-s50`

## Other Eval Runners

